WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.817
Container networking can make services easy to connect, but easy connectivity is not the same as trust.

2
00:00:06.817 --> 00:00:20.215
Exposed ports, published ports, service discovery names, ingress rules, load balancers, and reverse proxies define who can reach the application and which path a request follows before it reaches ASP.

3
00:00:20.215 --> 00:00:31.185
NET Core. A . NET service should clearly distinguish public APIs, admin paths, internal management endpoints, health endpoints, metrics, and user-facing traffic.

4
00:00:31.185 --> 00:00:38.393
Those paths may need different authentication, authorization, rate limits, logging, and response detail.

5
00:00:38.393 --> 00:00:47.795
A single container image may run in many environments, so exposure should be defined by deployment policy and application behavior together.

6
00:00:47.795 --> 00:00:53.280
TLS termination and proxy configuration affect application security decisions.

7
00:00:53.280 --> 00:01:08.402
Forwarded headers, authentication cookies, redirect behavior, secure headers, CORS, CSRF protections, client IP handling, and absolute URL generation can all depend on how ingress and reverse proxy settings are configured.

8
00:01:08.402 --> 00:01:13.574
Developers should know which headers are trusted and where TLS is terminated.

9
00:01:13.574 --> 00:01:18.667
Health checks should verify readiness without exposing sensitive details.

10
00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:30.576
They should tell the platform whether the service can receive traffic, not reveal configuration values, dependency secrets, internal paths, or detailed exception information.

11
00:01:30.576 --> 00:01:37.158
Network location alone should never be the only reason a caller is trusted to access data or actions.
